  "onboardingNext": "Next",
  "onboardingStart": "Get started",
  "onboarding1Title": "Sentinel watches over your phone",
  "onboarding1Line1": "A security score and the settings to fix.",
  "onboarding1Line2": "Suspicious apps: fake apps, spyware.",
  "onboarding1Line3": "Scam messages, analyzed on the phone.",
  "onboarding1Line4": "An encrypted vault for your passwords.",
  "onboarding1Line5": "A firewall that blocks dangerous sites and trackers.",
  "onboarding2Title": "Everything stays on your phone",
  "onboarding2Line1": "No account, no ads, no analytics. Your messages, your passwords and your list of apps never leave the phone.",
  "onboarding2Line2": "There are only three network exchanges, all optional, and only if you turn them on:",
  "onboarding2Line3": "• Checking for leaked passwords (Have I Been Pwned): 5 characters of a hash, never the password.",
  "onboarding2Line4": "• DNS firewall: the names of allowed sites are sent, encrypted, to the Quad9 resolver.",
  "onboarding2Line5": "• Blocklist updates: a simple download from GitHub.",
  "onboarding3Title": "Permissions, at the right time",
  "onboarding3Line1": "Sentinel doesn't ask for anything now.",
  "onboarding3Line2": "Each permission (notifications, notification access, VPN, biometrics) is requested only when you turn on the feature that needs it, with an explanation.",
  "onboarding3Line3": "You can turn everything off at any time.",
  "privacyBriefTitle": "In short",
  "privacyBrief1": "Sentinel has no server, no account, no ads, no usage analytics and no crash reports. All analyses run on the phone.",
  "privacyBrief2": "Sentinel only connects to the Internet for the three optional features below, and only after you agree.",
  "privacyNetworkTitle": "The only network exchanges (optional)",
  "privacyNetwork1": "1. Leaked password check (vault). After you agree, Sentinel computes the SHA-1 hash of each password and sends only its first 5 characters to Have I Been Pwned (api.pwnedpasswords.com), over HTTPS. The password and its full hash never leave the phone. The service sees your IP address. You can withdraw your consent; the results are then erased.",
  "privacyNetwork2": "2. DNS firewall. When you turn it on, the names of the sites requested by your apps go through Sentinel. Blocked sites get an answer on the phone; the other names are sent to the Quad9 resolver, encrypted (DNS over TLS or over HTTPS). Quad9 sees these names and your IP address, under its own privacy policy. If a network blocks encrypted DNS, nothing is sent unencrypted unless you allow it for that network.",
  "privacyNetwork3": "3. Blocklist updates. Off by default. If you start one or turn on automatic updates (at most once a day), Sentinel downloads the lists from GitHub (raw.githubusercontent.com), without cookies or any information about you. GitHub sees your IP address.",
  "privacyNetwork4": "\"Open the website\" links (advice, sources, audit report) open in your browser, only when you tap them.",
  "privacyLocalTitle": "What stays on the phone",
  "privacyLocal1": "The vault (passwords, notes): encrypted with your master password (Argon2id, AES-256-GCM).",
  "privacyLocal2": "Your choices: consent to the leak check, firewall settings and list of allowed sites, automatic protection on or off, introduction already seen, language, theme.",
  "privacyLocal3": "The blocklists and their updates.",
  "privacyLocal4": "\"Analyze an app\" reports: the list of sites contacted by the chosen app, erased at the latest 24 hours after the analysis.",
  "privacyLocal5": "Android backup is turned off: nothing goes to the cloud. A vault backup (encrypted .sentinel file) is only created if you ask for it, in the place you choose.",
  "privacyNeverTitle": "What Sentinel never records",
  "privacyNever1": "The text of analyzed messages and QR codes (pasted, shared, scanned or read from notifications): it is analyzed in memory and then forgotten. The camera image is never saved.",
  "privacyNever2": "Your browsing history: the firewall only keeps counters, never a site name (except during an app analysis that you start).",
  "privacyNever3": "The Wi-Fi name and your location: Sentinel doesn't ask for location access.",
  "privacyPermissionsTitle": "Permissions",
  "privacyPermissions1": "List of installed apps: to spot fake apps and spyware. It is never sent anywhere.",
  "privacyPermissions2": "Notifications: requested when you turn on automatic protection or the firewall, to show alerts.",
  "privacyPermissions3": "Notification access: an Android setting you open yourself for automatic protection; Sentinel reads incoming messages to look for scams.",
  "privacyPermissions4": "VPN: requested when you turn on the firewall. It only captures DNS, not the rest of the traffic.",
  "privacyPermissions5": "Biometrics: only if you choose to unlock the vault without typing your master password.",
  "privacyPermissions6": "Camera: requested when you tap “Scan a QR code”, only to read the QR code, on the phone.",
  "privacyEraseTitle": "Erasing your data",
  "privacyErase1": "Uninstalling Sentinel erases everything it saved on the phone. There is no copy anywhere else.",
  "auditFrameTitle": "Scope",
  "auditFrame1": "Security audit based on the OWASP MASVS v2 standard, from October 21 to 24, 2026, on Sentinel 1.0.0: review of the code, network traffic, storage and Android components.",
  "auditFrame2": "It was carried out for the app's owner, on their own code: it is not an independent audit.",
  "auditResultsTitle": "Results",
  "auditResults1": "17 findings, including one of high severity and four medium.",
  "auditResults2": "14 are fixed, each with an automated test. 3 are knowingly accepted (readable anti-scam model since the app is open source, no certificate pinning, one finding already compliant).",
  "auditResults3": "Since then, the firewall encrypts all its requests and the app no longer contacts anything without consent.",
  "auditLimitsTitle": "Limits",
  "auditLimits1": "No app is 100% safe. The main remaining risks depend on the phone: an Android version that is too old, or spyware that was given accessibility or root access.",
  "auditReadFull": "Read the full report on GitHub",
